<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=oracle-bone-script">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oracle bone script</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1250 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Jiaguwen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The oracle bone script is the oldest attested form of written Chinese, carved mostly on ox shoulder bones and turtle plastrons for the Late Shang royal family from the 13th century BCE; inscriptions of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-ding">Wu Ding</a>'s reign are radiocarbon dated to 1254–1197 BCE. Most of the estimated 150,000 inscriptions, found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yinxu">Yinxu</a>, the last Shang capital, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a>, record divinations. Already a mature system, with phonetic loans and phono-semantic compounds, it is the direct ancestor of the later Chinese scripts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oracle-bone-script.jpg" alt="Oracle bone script" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shang_Inscribed_Ox_Scapula_(for_divination).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The inscriptions were made by carving characters into oracle bones, usually the shoulder bones of oxen or the plastrons of turtles. They mainly record the results of official divinations for the Late Shang royal family. In these divinations, which took the form of scapulimancy, the bones were exposed to flames, creating patterns of cracks that were then interpreted. Both the prompt and the interpretation were inscribed on the bone used for the divination. Inscriptions range from 10 to over 100 characters, a few dozen being typical, and their subjects include war, ritual sacrifice and agriculture, as well as births, illnesses and deaths in the royal family. Each of the last nine Shang kings is named in them, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-ding">Wu Ding</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The tradition of writing behind the script is generally agreed to be older than the first known examples, since the attested script is already mature. Many characters had been simplified and made linear, and semantic extension and phonetic loaning had clearly been used for some time, perhaps for centuries. Yet no clearly identifiable writing earlier than the 13th century BCE has been found, and the symbols on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> pottery, jade and bone have not been shown to be ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a> script.</p><p>The bronze inscriptions, which keep a more pictorial style, are thought to be closer to typical Shang writing on bamboo books than the angular bone forms, which were simplified because the hard surface was difficult to engrave.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-yirong">Wang Yirong</a> recognised the characters as ancient Chinese writing in 1899. Liu E published the first collection of rubbings in 1903, and Luo Zhenyu identified the names of the Shang kings, which showed the bones to be Shang artefacts. Wang Guowei showed that the Shang kings' commemorative cycle matched the list in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s Records of the Historian, and Dong Zuobin established a chronology.</p><p>Over 30,000 distinct characters are found on the fragments, which may represent about 4,000 individual characters in their various forms. Scholars believe they can decipher between 1,500 and 2,000 of them. Some characters are attested only here, such as one for spring that has no known modern counterpart. After the Zhou overthrew the Shang, divination with milfoil became more common and far fewer inscriptions date to the Western Zhou, though a cache found in 1977 held thousands of Zhou-era bones, and an ancestral temple for Duke Dan of Zhou, uncovered in 2004, held over 2,000 inscriptions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oracle bone script” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1250 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Anyang, China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Its mature form implies earlier development, but no older writing has been found.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=oracle-bone-script">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
